Was this a scorpion in the toad era decision where you knew you were going to pay this off 15 episodes hence or did you come up with this later on?
这算是在写《蟾蜍与蝎子》那集时就决定好的，你知道要在15集后才揭晓，还是你后来才想到的？

00:39:17.460 --> 00:39:19.888
I think we came up with it somewhere in the middle.
我想我们大概是在中间某个时候想到的。

00:39:19.888 --> 00:39:28.459
I think we thought he was so shitty and scorpion in the toad and we did think there was some truth when he said like I'm just trying to get you toughened up for the world out here.
我觉得我们在写《蟾蜍与蝎子》时觉得他太混蛋了，而且他说的那些话——“我只是在帮你适应外面的世界”——我们确实觉得有几分道理。

00:39:28.459 --> 00:39:35.078
I think Barney told himself that and wanted to believe that but I think somewhere along the way we thought we need to pay this off somehow.
我想巴尼当时是这么对自己说的，也愿意相信这一点，但我觉得在后来的某个阶段，我们觉得得给这个伏笔一个交代。

00:39:35.078 --> 00:39:40.411
We need to show that Barney was instrumental in this and we knew there would be a battle if he was the best man.
我们需要展现巴尼在其中起到了关键作用，而且我们知道如果他要当伴郎，肯定会引发一番争夺。

00:39:40.411 --> 00:39:45.172
Barney wanted to be the best man and we thought what if Barney did actually earn being the best man.
巴尼想当伴郎，于是我们想，如果巴尼真的靠自己的表现赢得了伴郎之位会怎么样。

00:39:45.172 --> 00:39:54.219
So I think we didn't come up with this like five minutes before you know the table or read or something, but I don't know that we had it at Scorpion and the Chode, I think it was in between.
所以我觉得我们不是在剧本围读前五分钟才突然想到的，但我也说不准在《蟾蜍与蝎子》的时候有没有这个想法，我觉得应该是在那之后。

00:39:54.219 --> 00:40:02.340
There's also something, it's so dramatic and it's so, it's also like something, like you need a little money to do this.
而且还有一点，这非常戏剧化，而且很……怎么说呢，感觉你需要付出一点代价才能做到。

00:40:02.340 --> 00:40:14.679
Like there was something really, it tracked for me, like it made sense that he would do this and it opens him up and- And I love how immediately Ted is willing to share best man'smanship with Ted.
这对我来说很符合逻辑，他会这么做是说得通的，这也让他敞开了心扉——而且我太喜欢泰德立刻愿意跟泰德分享伴郎资格的反应了。

00:40:14.679 --> 00:40:15.519
Right, right, right.
对，对，对。

00:40:15.519 --> 00:40:18.119
Ted's the first guy that says, I think Barney's your best man.
泰德是第一个说出“我觉得巴尼是你的最佳伴郎人选”的人。

00:40:18.119 --> 00:40:19.039
Yeah, yeah.
对，对。

00:40:19.039 --> 00:40:20.360
It's a very generous thing to say.
这话显得非常大度。

00:40:20.360 --> 00:40:26.599
But there's also, there's two ways that you guys have constructed to deal with Barney's Barney-ness and sometimes horrible-ness.
但你们也用过两种方式来处理巴尼那种“巴尼式”的性格，以及有时让人讨厌的一面。

00:40:26.599 --> 00:40:31.000
One is to give him a come-up-ins, like with the twins, you know?
一种是给他点教训，比如双胞胎那次，你知道的？

00:40:31.000 --> 00:40:32.000
Yes.
对。

00:40:32.000 --> 00:40:37.300
Like to shame him, to kind of leave him alone at the end of the night and somewhat humiliated.
比如羞辱他一番，让他在深夜独自留下，多少有点颜面扫地。

00:40:37.300 --> 00:40:41.840
And the other way is to reveal a softer, sweeter side of him.
而另一种方式则是展现他较软弱、温柔的一面。

00:40:41.840 --> 00:40:42.840
Yes.
没错。

00:40:42.840 --> 00:40:43.840
Yes.
对。

00:40:43.840 --> 00:40:44.840
Yeah.
对。

00:40:44.840 --> 00:40:45.840
Yeah.
对。

00:40:45.840 --> 00:40:46.840
And it really played here.
这一集里确实很好地展现了这一点。

00:40:46.840 --> 00:40:49.480
and Lillie had this sort of sweet secret.
而且莉莉心里也藏着这样一个甜蜜的秘密。

00:40:49.480 --> 00:40:52.159
And I love that Lillie knew he was right.
我特别喜欢莉莉知道他是对的。

00:40:52.159 --> 00:40:54.800
Lillie didn't say a word in that scene, but what did she do?
莉莉在那个场景里一句话也没说，但她做了什么？

00:40:54.800 --> 00:41:02.280
She packed her stuff up and came back to New York and began the steps towards getting back together with Marshall, which is what this whole season has been about.
她收拾好行李回到了纽约，并开始迈出与马修复合的步伐，而这正是整季节目的核心。

00:41:02.280 --> 00:41:05.719
It's the Ted and Robin season and the Marshall and Lillie get back together season.
这是泰德与罗宾的季节，也是马修和莉莉复合的季节。

00:41:05.719 --> 00:41:11.980
And it turns out it all began in San Francisco before the season even started to really hurt.
事实证明，这一切甚至在这季正式开始前就已经在旧金山悄然萌芽了。

00:41:11.980 --> 00:41:16.619
Yeah, right, it happened over the summer between the seasons.
对，没错，是发生在两季之间的那个夏天。

00:41:16.619 --> 00:41:19.820
Barney wants to have been the guy that creates the story that you tell.
巴尼总是渴望成为那个缔造你们所讲述的故事的人。

00:41:19.820 --> 00:41:20.820
Yeah.
对。

00:41:20.820 --> 00:41:25.880
But you know what, a lot of parties are fueled by that guy.
但你知道吗，很多派对恰恰就是靠这种人来维持热度的。

00:41:25.880 --> 00:41:28.500
If it wasn't for that guy, there'd be less parties in the world.
要不是这种人，世界上的派对乐趣都要少很多。

00:41:28.500 --> 00:41:29.500
It's sweet taste of liberty.
那是自由的甜美滋味。

00:41:29.500 --> 00:41:30.500
It's a licking liberty bell.
就像舔一口自由钟。

00:41:30.500 --> 00:41:33.940
In the end of that is the story they told to their deathbed.
而这一切的结局，就是他们直到临终前都津津乐道的故事。

00:41:33.940 --> 00:41:35.900
Barney does create those memories.
巴尼确实创造了那些回忆。

00:41:35.900 --> 00:41:40.380
They're just sometimes terrible memories, but they become funny and kind of legendary over time.
只不过有时候是些糟糕的回忆，但随着时间推移，它们都变成了趣谈，甚至成了传奇。

00:41:40.380 --> 00:41:43.260
But you know what, if you're going to go for it, that's the risk.
但话又说回来，如果你想放手一搏，这就是你要承担的风险。

00:41:43.260 --> 00:41:44.260
Yeah.
对。

00:41:44.260 --> 00:41:46.820
And with this will be remembered somehow.
不管怎样，这段经历都会被铭记。

00:41:46.820 --> 00:41:48.780
We're good or bad, it will be remembered.
无论好坏，它都会被人记住。

00:41:48.780 --> 00:41:51.420
I want to thank you for mentioning the Liberty Bell.
我很感谢你提到了自由钟。

00:41:51.420 --> 00:41:53.820
We do have an ending to this whole section of the show.
我们节目这一段的内容也要告一段落了。

00:41:53.820 --> 00:42:02.320
I wanna talk for a second about a friend of ours who appears in the background of this episode.
我想花点时间聊聊一位出现在这集背景里的老朋友。

00:42:02.320 --> 00:42:07.360
Craig, I want you to tell us a little bit about who he is and how he at the Steakhouse.
克雷格，我想请你跟我们讲讲他是谁，以及他怎么会出现在牛排馆里的。

00:42:07.360 --> 00:42:11.239
Yes, we'd be remiss not to give the shout out at the Steakhouse scene.
对，如果在牛排馆的场景里不特地提一下他，那可就太遗憾了。

00:42:11.239 --> 00:42:15.440
First of all, there are a lot of caricatures of hemiumers up on the wall mixed in with celebrities.
首先，墙上挂着很多弗吉尼亚人的漫画画像，跟名人的画像混在一起。

00:42:15.440 --> 00:42:24.119
There's Carter, there's me, there's some other people, then there's like Conan O'Brien and weird, but there's an actual real life for human, and that's the end too.
有卡特，有我，还有其他人，然后还有像柯南·奥布莱恩，挺奇妙的，但其中有一个是现实生活中的活人，那就是诺亚。

00:42:24.119 --> 00:42:30.119
In the background, eating dinner is Noah Garrison, who we went to college with, Alec and I at Wesleyan.
在背景里吃晚餐的那位是诺亚·加里森，亚历克和我当年在卫斯理大学的同窗。

00:42:30.119 --> 00:42:31.880
Let's hear from Noah.
让我们听听诺亚怎么说。

00:42:31.880 --> 00:42:44.800
Let's hear from Noah Garrison, who would go on to his life, would go on to be an important part of Marshall's life, of your creation of the Marshall character, in terms of a lawyer, let's hear what Noah has to say.
让我们听听诺亚·加里森的讲述。他后来的人生，将成为马修生活中的重要部分——也是你们在构思马修这个律师角色时的原型，听听诺亚怎么说吧。

00:42:44.800 --> 00:42:48.880
You have an audio clip of Noah that you almost forgot about, but just remember, does that happen?
你手里有一段诺亚的音频，你差点忘了，但刚刚又想起来了，是这样吗？

00:42:48.880 --> 00:42:49.719
Maybe.
也许吧。

00:42:49.719 --> 00:42:51.360
Here we go.
我们这就听听。

00:42:51.360 --> 00:42:52.440
Hi, everyone.
大家好。

00:42:52.440 --> 00:42:56.960
Josh and Craig, hello, and Alec, thanks for the introduction.
乔希和克雷格，你们好，亚历克，谢谢你的介绍。

00:42:56.960 --> 00:42:58.599
Sort of bringing this all together.
这算是把这一切都串联起来了。

00:42:58.599 --> 00:43:03.880
I met Alec in nursery school in New York when we were three years old.
我三岁的时候就在纽约的幼儿园认识了亚历克。

00:43:03.880 --> 00:43:06.559
We ended up together through summer camp.
我们后来一起参加夏令营。

00:43:06.559 --> 00:43:07.800
We went to high school together.
我们一起上高中。

00:43:07.800 --> 00:43:17.800
We went to college together, and that's where I then met Craig, literally on our first day at Wesley and Craig, you lived across the hall from me.
我们一起上大学，也就是在那里我认识了克雷格—— literalmente（字面意思上）就在我们去卫斯理的第一天，克雷格，你就住在我的宿舍斜对面。

00:43:17.800 --> 00:43:19.320
And so we hung out a lot our freshman year.
所以我们大一的时候经常一起玩。

00:43:19.320 --> 00:43:23.480
And I think Alec has already taken credit for the best part of your life.
我想亚历克大概已经把“你人生中最棒的部分”的功劳揽到他自己头上了。

00:43:23.480 --> 00:43:33.559
But I really want to reiterate that the entire reason Craig met his really wonderful wife Rebecca is that she'd come up to visit us, which then led to the two of them meeting.
但我真的要重申，克雷格能遇到他非常出色的妻子丽贝卡，全因她当时跑来探望我们，这随后才促成了两人的相识。

00:43:33.559 --> 00:43:38.880
And really Craig was just a lucky bystander and everything that has then led to all of this.
说到底，克雷格只是这一切发生时一个幸运的旁观者，后面的种种发展其实跟他本人没太大关系。

00:43:38.880 --> 00:43:40.840
none of this had to do with him personally.
这些都跟他个人没有必然联系。

00:43:40.840 --> 00:43:55.920
But connecting back to the show, Josh, I'm not sure if you'll remember this, but pretty early on after the pilot shot, but before it aired, I met up one night with you and Craig and Carter and Jason at a bar in LA and we all ended up hanging out together for a few hours.
不过扯回节目本身，乔希，我不确定你还记不记得，但在试播集拍摄后不久、正式播出前，有一天晚上我和你、克雷格、卡特还有杰森在洛杉矶的一家酒吧碰面了，我们一伙人在那儿一起玩了好几个小时。

00:43:55.920 --> 00:44:00.280
You know, you were all just a really genuinely friendly and fun group to hang out with.
你知道的，你们所有人都是那种真诚友好、相处起来非常开心的朋友。

00:44:00.280 --> 00:44:13.960
But the thing I remember most about that night was that after a few hours, we all got like super amped up that we were gonna pair tabs, grab her things and head straight out to go to Coachella for the rest of the weekend to the music festival out there.
但我对那天晚上印象最深的是，过了几个小时后，我们突然超级兴奋地一拍即合，决定结账、拿上东西，直接驱车前往科切拉音乐节，去赶那个周末剩下的音乐节活动。

00:44:13.960 --> 00:44:33.159
And after the first wave of how utterly awesome this was gonna be hit, I also immediately remembered in the just most deflating way possible that I was a first year law student, literally in the middle of studying for final exams, and there was absolutely no way I could get in a car with all of you.
就在这股“这主意简直酷毙了”的冲动袭来之后，我又极其沮丧地猛然想起：我是一名大一法学专业学生，正处在备战期末考试的焦头烂额之中，我绝对没有可能跟你们一起挤上一辆车。

00:44:34.440 --> 00:44:41.840
I don't remember if you guys actually did go, but I'm now rewatching the show with my son who is 10 years old and completely loves it.
我不记得你们最后到底去没去，不过我现在正和十岁的儿子一起重温这部剧，他超级喜欢。

00:44:41.840 --> 00:44:48.679
And I realized that early in season one, there's an episode where Ted and Barney take off and go to lick the Liberty Bell.
我突然意识到，在第一季刚开始的时候，有一集是泰德和巴尼抛下手头的事去舔自由钟。

00:44:48.679 --> 00:44:51.199
And Marshall gets left at home studying for law school.
而马修则被留在家里准备法学院考试。

00:44:51.199 --> 00:44:54.920
And in hindsight, that just hits a little bit too close to home for me.
事后回想起来，这对我来说实在有点太戳心了。

00:44:54.920 --> 00:45:02.719
So I can't say though that 20 years later, I am absolutely certain I made the wrong call and I should have gotten in the car with all of you.
所以我必须说，20年后的今天，我无比确定我当年做了个错误的决定，我当时真应该跟你们一起上车的。

00:45:02.719 --> 00:45:04.639
Anyway though, this is great to see.
不过不管怎样，看到这些真的很棒。

00:45:04.639 --> 00:45:08.719
I'm so happy to have gotten a cameo early in the show and in a really fun episode.
我很高兴能在剧集刚开播时客串一把，而且还是在一集这么有趣的剧集里。

00:45:08.719 --> 00:45:14.519
And I'm really excited for you guys in the podcast and that I'm getting to rewatch such a totally wonderful show.
我真的为你们的播客感到兴奋，我也很高兴能有机会重温这样一部绝对精彩的剧集。

00:45:14.519 --> 00:45:15.360
Oh my God.
天哪。

00:45:15.360 --> 00:45:17.440
Wow, thanks Noah.
哇，谢谢诺亚。

00:45:17.440 --> 00:45:19.280
Josh, did you and Carter do it?
乔什，你和卡特去过吗？

00:45:19.280 --> 00:45:20.280
I think you did go to Coachella.
我想你们确实去过科切拉音乐节。

00:45:20.280 --> 00:45:21.119
We did.
我们去过。

00:45:21.119 --> 00:45:23.400
I went two years in a row, I think, with Carter.
我想我连续两年和卡特一起去了。

00:45:23.400 --> 00:45:27.320
Me and three, I think Carter and I went to Coachella at least twice together.
我和三……我觉得卡特和我至少一起去过两次科切拉。

00:45:27.320 --> 00:45:32.920
You went in your one because that's where you discovered block party and the song, This Modern Love that we put on the finale of season one or two.
你去的那次是因为你在那里发现了Bloc Party乐队，还有那首我们在第一季或第二季大结局里用过的歌《This Modern Love》。

00:45:32.920 --> 00:45:35.039
I had to ask Carter.
我得去问问卡特。

00:45:35.039 --> 00:45:36.840
I know the whole reason we knew that song is.
我知道我们知道这首歌的全部原因是因为……

00:45:36.840 --> 00:45:38.599
Oh, it's because Block Party was there.
哦，因为Bloc Party在那里。

00:45:38.599 --> 00:45:41.480
You guys saw Block Party and you went, that song's fucking awesome.
你们看到Bloc Party然后说，这首歌太酷了。

00:45:41.480 --> 00:45:43.199
Cardi gives you credit for that, I think.
我觉得卡迪把这记在你的功劳上了。

00:45:43.199 --> 00:45:47.840
I also saw the shout out louds, which are a Swedish band that I put in Happy Thinking More.
我还看了The Shout Out Louds，那是一支瑞典乐队，我把它放进了《老爸老妈的浪漫史》。

00:45:47.840 --> 00:45:48.840
Please, the opening credits.
拜托，还有片头。

00:45:48.840 --> 00:45:49.715
I saw them.
我看到他们了。

00:45:49.715 --> 00:45:57.119
That was the first band I saw on like one of the days and it was really, it was just awesome.
那是某一天我看到的第一支乐队，真的很棒，棒极了。

00:45:57.119 --> 00:45:58.679
But Block Party was huge that year.
但当年Bloc Party非常火。

00:45:58.679 --> 00:46:01.760
I remember they were really a presence at Coachella.
我记得他们在科切拉音乐节上非常有存在感。

00:46:01.760 --> 00:46:03.360
A card I didn't know until you guys wanted that.
直到你们点播这首歌之前卡迪都不知道。

00:46:03.360 --> 00:46:04.360
So you guys went and did it.
所以你们真的去了。

00:46:04.360 --> 00:46:07.639
Noah studied for his test and you guys got amazing memories.
诺亚温习了功课，而你们留下了美好的回忆。

00:46:07.639 --> 00:46:08.639
And Noah, remember that?
诺亚，还记得吗？

00:46:08.639 --> 00:46:16.880
And Noah is the reason that Marshall is a lawyer and an environmental lawyer.
诺亚就是马歇尔成为一名律师兼环境法律师的原因。

00:46:16.880 --> 00:46:22.079
Noah is both a geologist and a lawyer and teaches environmental law, teaches at UCLA now.
诺亚既是地质学家又是律师，现在在加州大学洛杉矶分校（UCLA）教授环境法。

00:46:22.079 --> 00:46:23.079
He works for NRDC.
他在自然资源保护委员会（NRDC）工作。

00:46:23.079 --> 00:46:29.519
So a lot of the DNA of Marshall's kind of career and parts of who he is, I borrowed from Noah.
所以马歇尔的职业生涯和他的部分性格DNA，其实都是我从诺亚身上借鉴来的。

00:46:29.519 --> 00:46:33.079
Thank you, Noah, for allowing me to me and Carter to read your life the way we did.
谢谢你，诺亚，谢谢你允许我和卡特以那种方式“参考”你的人生。

00:46:33.079 --> 00:46:38.139
And if this timeline is correct that we were drink out drinking probably at the cat in the fiddle.
如果时间线没错的话，我们当时可能正泡在“小提琴猫”酒吧里喝酒。

00:46:38.139 --> 00:46:40.579
We were out drinking pint after pint.
我们一杯接一杯地喝着啤酒。

00:46:40.579 --> 00:46:46.619
And then in the morning going to drive out to Coachella just shows how young we were and how resilient our bodies were.
然后第二天早上开车去科切拉，这正好说明了我们当年是多么年轻，身体是多么抗造。

00:46:46.619 --> 00:46:52.900
And resilient idea that I take off her Coachella in the morning is that's another human spot.
而且一想到早上要出发去科切拉，那简直是另一个“人类禁区”。

00:46:52.900 --> 00:47:01.039
I have a headache thinking about just thinking about it.
光是想想，我都觉得头疼。

00:47:01.039 --> 00:47:02.039
All right.
好了。

00:47:02.039 --> 00:47:03.039
Welcome back.
欢迎回来。

00:47:03.039 --> 00:47:05.880
We're winding things up for this episode.
我们要为这一集做个收尾了。

00:47:05.880 --> 00:47:20.000
If you would like to share with us why you love how I met your mother, how it came into your life, how it changed your life, please go to how we made your mother dot com H W M Y M dot com.
如果你想和我们分享为什么你喜欢《老爸老妈的浪漫史》、它是如何进入你的生活、又是如何改变了你的人生，请访问 howwemadyourmother.com，即 HWMYM.com。

00:47:20.000 --> 00:47:29.840
Go to contact and just follow the instructions so you can either write us a letter which we might read at the end of an episode or you can record a voice note which we might play at the beginning of the episode.
点击“联系我们”，按照说明操作：你可以给我们写一封信，我们可能会在节目末尾读到它；或者你可以录下一段语音留言，我们可能会在节目开头播放。

00:47:29.840 --> 00:47:35.800
It's one of our favorite things about doing the show is hearing from all of you about what The show's meant to you.
做这个节目最让我们喜爱的事情之一，就是听到你们大家讲述这部剧对你们意味着什么。

00:47:35.800 --> 00:47:38.440
And how it made you feel about your name, please?
以及它如何让你对你的名字……请问？

00:47:38.440 --> 00:47:40.159
Yeah, and how it helped you acclimate to your.
对，还有它如何帮助你适应你的……

00:47:40.159 --> 00:47:44.360
So we want to hear from some Ted, some Barney's, some Marshall's, and some Lillies, do you even think so?
所以我们想听到一些泰德、巴尼、马歇尔和莉莉的声音，你觉得呢？

00:47:44.360 --> 00:47:46.079
Yeah, yeah.
对，对。

00:47:46.079 --> 00:47:50.599
So this is a great letter we got from India, and I am going to read it now.
这是我们收到的一封来自印度的很棒的信，我现在就要读一下。

00:47:50.599 --> 00:47:57.719
Hey, Craig, Josh, and Alec, I'm Abhishek, and I'm from Mumbai, India.
嘿，克雷格、乔什和亚历克，我是阿布舍克，来自印度孟买。

00:47:57.719 --> 00:48:08.840
It's been a while since I've been listening to your podcast, and honestly, I'm not a podcast guy, but since the very first episode, There's nothing I look forward to more every week than the whimium podcast.
我听你们的播客有一段时间了，说实话，我平时不怎么听播客，但自从第一期节目以来，每周最让我期待的事就是收听《老爸老妈的浪漫史》播客（WHMYM Podcast）。

00:48:08.840 --> 00:48:15.800
Maybe it's because ever since the show ended, I've been trying to hold on to it in any capacity that I could and for good reason.
也许是因为自从这部剧完结后，我就一直想尽一切办法留住它，而且理由充分。

00:48:15.800 --> 00:48:23.079
2014, the year of the show ended, I graduated high school and just like that, two of my favorite things in life no longer existed.
2014年，也就是这部剧完结的那一年，我高中毕业了，就这样，我生活中的两件最爱同时消失了。

00:48:23.079 --> 00:48:27.760
My friends from school and my friends on TV were suddenly not around.
我在学校的朋友和电视里的朋友突然都不在身边了。

00:48:27.760 --> 00:48:30.320
I missed how I met your mother and I missed school.
我想念《老爸老妈的浪漫史》，我想念学校。

00:48:30.320 --> 00:48:33.559
And there were no substitutes for those two.
这两样东西都没有替代品。

00:48:33.559 --> 00:48:46.400
On top of that, I was off to college, majoring in something I had no interest in, because Indian parents always, almost always, turned to engineering or medicine after school before they let you do what you want to do.
最重要的是，我要去上大学了，主修一个我毫无兴趣的专业，因为印度的父母几乎总是让你在高中毕业后去学工程或医学，然后才允许你去做你想做的事。

00:48:46.400 --> 00:48:51.960
And thus began four gruesome years of my life, and the only thing that kept me going was how I met your mother.
于是，我生命中残酷的四年开始了，唯一支撑我坚持下去的就是《老爸老妈的浪漫史》。

00:48:51.960 --> 00:48:56.920
I watched it again and again and again for four years until I was done with college.
在大学四年的时间里，我一遍又一遍、一遍又一遍地看，直到大学毕业。

00:48:56.920 --> 00:49:04.840
I didn't hate my time there and it wasn't bad, but in hindsight, I've started appreciating that time because I've realized now that I was supposed to be there.
我并不讨厌那段时光，它也并不糟糕，但事后回想起来，我开始感激那段时光，因为我意识到，我当时就该去那里。

00:49:04.840 --> 00:49:13.440
I realized precisely what the show was trying to say when Ted said that you end up exactly where you're supposed to be, exactly when you're supposed to be there.
我终于明白了这部剧真正想表达的意思——就像泰德说的，你最终会去到你该去的地方，在恰当的时间。

00:49:13.440 --> 00:49:14.800
And I'll tell you why.
我来告诉你们为什么。

00:49:14.800 --> 00:49:21.159
I'll tell you about the butterfly effect that transpired in my life because it was indeed like destiny.
我来跟你们讲讲在我生命中发生的蝴蝶效应，因为这确实就像命中注定一样。

00:49:21.159 --> 00:49:26.199
After I was done with those four years, I joined a design school to finally do what I'd always wanted to do.
度过那四年之后，我进入了一所设计学校，终于去做了我一直想做的事。

00:49:26.199 --> 00:49:29.800
And it was there that I met my best friend and now girlfriend.
正是在那里，我遇到了我的挚友，也就是我现在的女朋友。

00:49:29.800 --> 00:49:34.719
We've known each other for seven years and have been together for the last three and a half.
我们认识七年了，在一起三年半了。

00:49:34.719 --> 00:49:38.000
I've never been more certain about anything than spending my life with her.
我从来没有对任何事情如此笃定，就像我笃定要和她共度余生一样。

00:49:38.000 --> 00:49:50.000
I'm completely in love with her and even though she doesn't understand my obsession with how I met your mother, rolling her eyes every time Ted starts talking about the one, she knows the impact the show has had in shaping me.
我深深地爱着她，尽管她并不理解我对《老爸老妈的浪漫史》的痴迷——每当泰德开始讲起“真爱”时她就会翻白眼——但她知道这部剧对塑造现在的我有着怎样的影响。

00:49:50.000 --> 00:49:59.480
So had I not majored in engineering and instead gone to some design school four years earlier, I probably would not have met her and would still be cursing my time after high school.
所以，如果我当年没有主修工程学，而是四年前直接去了设计学校，我可能就不会遇到她，而且现在大概还在痛骂高中毕业后的那段时光。

00:49:59.480 --> 00:50:11.780
This realization made such an impact on me that for my senior dissertation, I wrote a paper on how I met your mother and the causal significance of seemingly unimportant moments and decisions in our lives, the butterfly effect.
这种顿悟对我的影响太深了，以至于在我的大学毕业论文里，我写了一篇关于《老爸老妈的浪漫史》以及我们生活中那些看似不重要却具有决定性意义的瞬间和决定的文章——也就是蝴蝶效应。

00:50:11.780 --> 00:50:13.940
It was my way of saying, thank you.
这是我表达感谢的方式。

00:50:13.940 --> 00:50:18.820
An ode to the series for making me understand that you can't jump straight to the end.
向这部剧致敬，因为它让我明白你不能直接跳到结局。

00:50:18.820 --> 00:50:21.260
The journey is the best part.
旅程才是最美好的部分。

00:50:21.260 --> 00:50:25.380
Today, like Ted, I get to do what I've always wanted to do as a kid.
如今，就像泰德一样，我终于能够从事我从小就一直想做的工作了。

00:50:25.380 --> 00:50:28.179
I sketch and design cars for a living.
我以画画和设计汽车为生。

00:50:28.179 --> 00:50:37.070
And like Ted, who got to design the Spokane National Bank Tower at a young age, the first car I've designed is all set to launch next year.
就像当年年轻的泰德设计了斯波坎国家银行大楼一样，我设计的第一辆汽车也将在明年全面推出。

00:50:37.070 --> 00:50:50.150
I hope I get to live the dream with my girlfriend by my side, and it's all because of that one decision that was forced on me by my folks after high school, so I could be at the right place, at the right time.
我希望能在女朋友的陪伴下实现这个梦想，这一切都归功于当年高中毕业后父母强加给我的那个决定，它让我能在对的时间出现在对的地方。

00:50:50.150 --> 00:50:52.590
I cannot thank you guys enough for making how I met your mother.
对你们创作了《老爸老妈的浪漫史》，我感激不尽。

00:50:52.590 --> 00:50:54.349
It's not just a show to me.
对我来说，它不仅仅是一部剧。

00:50:54.349 --> 00:50:56.949
It's a guidebook to living life.
它是生活指南。

00:50:56.949 --> 00:50:57.980
That's awesome.
太棒了。

00:50:57.980 --> 00:50:58.980
Man.
老兄。

00:50:58.980 --> 00:50:59.980
Great letter.
好信件。

00:50:59.980 --> 00:51:00.980
Congrats on that car.
恭喜你设计了那辆车。

00:51:00.980 --> 00:51:01.980
Can it be called the Mosby?
它能叫“莫斯比”号吗？

00:51:01.980 --> 00:51:02.980
Just asking.
我随便问问。

00:51:02.980 --> 00:51:03.980
It hasn't gone to press yet.
还没投产呢。

00:51:03.980 --> 00:51:04.980
Can we still get it in there?
我们现在还能把这个名字塞进去吗？

00:51:04.980 --> 00:51:05.980
That's so wonderful.
太美妙了。

00:51:05.980 --> 00:51:07.980
Thank you for that letter.
谢谢你寄来这封信。

00:51:07.980 --> 00:51:20.980
And again, we're just so thrilled that this show we made has been such an important part of people's lives.
再次感谢，我们非常激动我们制作的这部剧能成为人们生活中如此重要的一部分。

00:51:20.980 --> 00:51:21.980
So thank you.
所以谢谢你们。

00:51:21.980 --> 00:51:22.980
Keep those letters coming.
请继续给我们写信。

00:51:22.980 --> 00:51:23.980
Keep those voice notes coming.
请继续发来语音留言。

00:51:23.980 --> 00:51:25.980
And we will see you next time.
我们下期再见。

